    "leafs": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Maple Leafs",
      "league": "NHL",
      "record": "32-36-14",
      "detail": "8th in Atlantic Division",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Leafs",
        "body": "The season ended the way too many Leafs seasons do - with more questions than answers, and a familiar ache hanging over the rink. <strong>Toronto finished 32-36-14, eighth in the Atlantic Division and 15th overall, a flat, hard number that says the club spent too much time chasing the game instead of driving it.</strong> The 78 points and a seventh straight loss at the end of the line underlined a team that never found a clean identity, never found enough finishing, and never stopped leaking goals at the wrong moments. That is the crime of this year: not one spectacular collapse, but a long, grinding fade. The front office now has to decide what stays and what goes - coaching, veterans, cap space, and whether the core deserves another turn or a hard reset. There is no sugarcoating the work ahead. The Leafs are not a contender with a few tweaks. They are a club that needs sharper judgment, fresher legs, and a different kind of edge. <strong>What matters now is the summer - the coaching review, the roster purge, and whether management finally chooses change over comfort.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated June 10, 2026"
      },
