    "jays": {
      "full_name": "Toronto Blue Jays",
      "league": "MLB",
      "record": "33-35",
      "detail": "3rd in AL East",
      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Jays",
        "body": "A club that was one bad bounce from another dispiriting loss found a jolt in the ninth and, for once, left the yardarm swinging its way. <strong>Brandon Valenzuela's walk-off single gave Toronto a 3-2 win over the Phillies on June 9</strong>, and suddenly a night that looked ordinary became the kind of push a club in search of footing needs. The Blue Jays are 33-35, still 3rd in the AL East, but the pulse is there - a one-game streak, and just enough life to keep the standings from hardening into something uglier. Valenzuela's first career walk-off, plus a career-high four hits, made him the story of the morning, while Toronto's recent stretch has been more honest than glamorous: two wins in three against Philadelphia, and three victories in four overall. That is not a juggernaut. It is not dead either. The larger issue remains whether this lineup can build on the scrape-and-claw energy or drift back into the same familiar dip. <strong>Next comes Wednesday night's rematch with Philadelphia at 6:07 PM ET - another test of whether this win was a spark or just a bright flicker.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated June 10, 2026"
      },
