      "lotl": {
        "label": "What's Going On With the Commanders",
        "body": "The spring has turned into a proving ground in Ashburn - less pageantry, more evaluation, and plenty of quiet pressure. <strong>The most important development is the Commanders' work in OTAs and minicamp, where the staff is sorting out new faces, new roles, and which young players can handle the weight of real expectations.</strong> Washington sits 3rd in the NFC East, 6 games behind, with a 1-game winning streak on the ledger and a 14th-place playoff seed in the league picture, but those numbers matter less now than the shape of the roster being hammered out day by day. This is where a team learns whether its draft picks can contribute before the leaves turn, whether the scheme changes fit the quarterback and protect him, and whether the depth chart has any hard edges at all. The most revealing story in June is usually the least glamorous one: who is lining up where, who is taking the second-team reps, and who looks like he belongs when the pace picks up. <strong>Watch the battles at the back end of the roster and the installation work through minicamp - because that is where Washington's real season begins.</strong>",
        "updated": "Updated June 10, 2026"
      },
